course: dictionary
lesson: 2073
title: Learning a word from its context
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A constellation is a group of stars that people see as a pattern or picture in the night sky, and they give it a name. The sentence around the word leaves a clue: «Groups of stars that make pictures in the sky are called constellations.» The clue says the sky, so the meaning is about stars.
explain: The word can also mean a group of related things or people. The sentence around it tells you which meaning is used. If the sentence is about the sky, it means stars. If it is about a set of linked things or people, it means that group.
explain: «The Southern Cross, Crux, is the smallest of the 88 constellations.» Swap in your meaning: "The Southern Cross is the smallest of the 88 star patterns" still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A constellation is a group of stars that people see as a pattern or picture in the night sky.
fact: «The stars of a constellation are usually at very different distances from us.» So the stars in one pattern are not all the same distance away.
fact: «Since 1922 to 1930 astronomers have used 88 official constellations covering the whole sky.»
fact: People in the past used constellations to find their way and to tell the seasons.
fact: «The evening constellations change through the year» because the Earth moves round the Sun, so at night we face a different part of space in each season.
exercise: «Groups of stars that make pictures in the sky are called constellations.» What is a constellation? | A telescope used by astronomers | A cloud that hides the stars | A planet that moves round the Sun | *A group of stars seen as a pattern or picture in the sky
exercise: «The stars of a constellation are usually at very different distances from us.» What does this tell you? | Constellations can only be seen in summer | Constellations are made of planets | *Stars in one pattern are often not the same distance from us | All stars in a constellation are the same distance away
exercise: How many official constellations have astronomers used since 1922 to 1930? | 22 | *88 | 12 | 1930
test: «The evening constellations change through the year.» Why do they change? | Constellations are made new each year | The stars move closer to Earth each night | Clouds cover different parts of the sky | *The Earth moves round the Sun, so we face a different part of space each season
test: A constellation can also mean a group of related things or people. Which sentence uses it that way? | The sky was full of bright stars | The telescope was pointed at the moon | The planet spins once a day | *The club is a constellation of friends who all love puzzles
exam: «The evening constellations change through the year.» Which meaning fits best? | A ship that sails at night | *A group of stars seen as a pattern in the night sky | A type of hot sunlight | A small, lost planet
